/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/w26-floor-lane.mem:134:* ''The floor card's freshness.'' The browser verifies the lineage, the head release and the floor bytes from the Nexus AID alone (`browser-floor-read`). The AID rides the same-origin trust line, and the signed card (`W14`), the pin's causal position (`W15`) and the carry-floor convergence (`W16`) stay owed.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/mesh-goldens.mem:36:# ''An event journal per vessel.'' Typed, append-only, durable entries: readiness phases, a bag projected, a carriage fold, floor pins filed or refused, a crossing, a refusal, a verb's own receipt. Ids run monotonic within a genesis epoch, so a re-genesis opens a new lineage; a writer prefix keeps two daemons on one store apart; compaction bounds its size. Much of it stands today as log lines (`[carriage] … place floor pin(s) …`, the verb dispatcher's readiness) and as `ReadinessMap` in mesh `readiness.ts`; the journal makes them data. It reads over the verb seat (a `lares events --follow --since <cursor>` verb) and in process as an async iterator.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/wiki-layer-ontology.mem:125:`lares` and `lararium` remain system bag names and recipe members. Only each Nexus's Kahu cabal writes them live. Every other wiki mounts them read-only through `@oracle`, as the floor CID its Nexus last released (operator ruling, 2026-10-16; <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road">> #/rulings, row `W26`). The release rides the crossroads board as the Nexus's public answer. A vessel moves its floor pin only on accepting a newer signed release, face by face. Their per-vessel document pointers still ride `@oracle` until that build lands; moving them to bootstrap seeds and each vessel's catalog remains owed on Kahu Plane. A leaf learns the admitted canon documents through its admit. A face's planes and the daemon URL resolve from that vessel's bootstrap alone.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/controller-handoff.mem:129:* ''Train W'': the full route pin, the sealed application frame, `GateSessionChannel` after the verdict, face-scoped sharing, the fleet-shadow consult and the assembled socket cutover stand. Owed: a signed floor card, the floor pin's causal position, two-vault carry, face switching on one device, a fleet-join carry walk in the browser weld, and the founder's own carriage fold, which reads its herms' floor pins as uncarried (held-out herm-mesh and the nexus cell).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/controller-handoff.mem:138:** Wave two: the rest of D2 on the island protocol (the port kinds, the invoke gate, presence); `B1`'s boot unlock and passkey, which unparks `--persona-root-seal`; the wire's owed rows `W14`, `W15`, `W16`, `W21` and `W22`; `W26` (the system wikis release on the crossroads and every other wiki reads the floor through `@oracle`), with `W21` riding it; `W25` (the carried bag event's creator in the clear) lands before wave three.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/controller-handoff.mem:147:** ''Slot one'', Wave D (`docs/pono/k15-wave-d-lane`, branch `--k15-wave-d`): slices 3 to 6, the tree-key rename and the canon sweep stand green on its CI and held-out. Slices 7 and 8 build under the operator's ruling of 2026-10-25: the seat lands when the grant record arrives (one serialized seat act, woken by the PersonaGroup change, the lease-prefix change and one boot replay; `faceSeated()` reads only; the lazy reuse ask burns), and the vocabulary check's scout exemption goes line by line through `evergreen-boundary`'s named-exception shape. Then the controller trains Wave D, and K17-R (`docs/pono/k17-realm-lease-lane`) follows on its lease ground.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/controller-handoff.mem:148:** ''Slot two'', lane `w28-gate`: `W10`'s research pair on the knock's leak, then `W28`'s frame freeze, since both move the dial's bytes once before the bake. A W10 reading that lands as an accepted leak goes to the operator. Then `W14` to `W16`.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/research-report-identity-as-dyad-person-devi-f55eee.mem:63:**KERI** (Sam Smith, arXiv 1907.02143, "Key Event Receipt Infrastructure") is a different and arguably more rigorous model: identity is a **self-certifying identifier bound to a key-event log** (append-only, signed transfer statements, pre-rotation, witnessed for duplicity detection). This is not quite "identity as an edge between two parties" — it's "identity as a *history*," verified independently by any witness, with **direct mode** (1:1 relationships via verified signatures) and **indirect mode** (1:many via witnessed receipts) as two separate trust postures layered on top. So KERI keeps identity subject-centric (one controller, one log) but makes trust *relational at the verification layer*. Citation count on the arXiv paper is only **2** (OpenAlex) — this is a spec/protocol effort, not an academic one, so citation count undercounts real influence (it lives inside Trust-over-IP governance work instead), but it is a low signal for independent scholarly uptake. (Confidence: medium.)
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/heraldry.mem:196:* ''CURE'': the harness lines above (`tests/harness/instance.ts:311-349`) plus the herm's explicit join (`nexus carry-for`, `nexus carry --floor-pin`, `nexus carry-take`), so its floor gate stands at A's Nexus (`herm-explicit-join.test.ts`, `herm-carriage.ts`).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/recovery-custody-scout.mem:157:* ''CIVIC scale'' — ⚠ ''corrected on a second read''. The charter ROTATION does not reconstruct anything: `sealRotate` seats the key-set that STANDS and verifies its digest against the head's `nextKeyCommit`, fail-closed. That is ''pre-rotation with a key-set reveal'' (TUF≈KERI), neither fork. ''Fork A's Shamir sits one layer beneath'' — `splitReserveSeed` 2-of-3 custodies the RESERVE SEED, the material `deriveReserveKeySet` turns into the successor key-set. So the 2-of-3 recovers ''the pre-committed future'', never the rotation authority.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:262:# ''Node private place signing seed'' (ruled 2026-10-19: a scoped online signer under a pre-rotated place KEL whose next key rides the founding card, built in the founding bake; the options it settled follow). Current `.vessel-key*.json` contains a private signing seed in a floor file and `loadVesselSigningSeed` reads it without a human route (`vault-carriers.ts:240-242`; `node-vessel-identity.ts:429-442`). ''Option A:'' keep a *named floor signer* owned by the node, protected at rest under an explicit mechanism; floor means no custody S key/FCK, while this narrowly scoped signer can support faceless Herm startup. Specify the protection, allowed signing/derivation domains, and restart behavior. ''Option B:'' put that signer behind an explicit boot custody door; public card/relay can stand before unlock, while operations requiring its signature wait/refuse until the door opens. Specify Herm boot and unattended container behavior. Neither option is selected here. The private seed is never F, never the current S key, and never the empty serving SK.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/research-findings-what-the-field-has-measure-cde3fc.mem:92:- Countervailing evidence — **"Building State Capacity: Evidence from Biometric Smartcards in India," American Economic Review 2016** (verified abstract, OpenAlex W1543347785): a large randomized rollout across **157 subdistricts, 19 million people** in Andhra Pradesh found biometric smartcards delivered *faster, more predictable, less corrupt* payments **without adversely affecting program access**, and beneficiaries overwhelmingly preferred the new system. **The field disagrees with itself here**, and the disagreement tracks implementation quality (rollout management), not the underlying biometric-identity concept — report both sides as instructed.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/topic-a-the-onboarding-cost-question-1c7848.mem:283:## B.5 MEASURED — KERI pre-rotation: the model that has no overlap window
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/topic-a-the-onboarding-cost-question-1c7848.mem:291:- **Recovery via superseding rotation:** "a rotation event may be used to supersede an interaction event. When this happens the key event log **forks** at the superseding rotation event." For delegated identifiers this "enable[s] recovery even in the event of the **joint compromise** of a delegating identifier's signing keys and the delegated identifier's pre-rotated keys," and nests to multiple delegation levels.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/topic-a-the-onboarding-cost-question-1c7848.mem:293:**Why this matters for you:** pre-rotation is the one design in this survey that **eliminates the dual-authority window by construction**. There is no period where two authorities are both live, because the successor authority is *committed but hidden* in advance and the KEL's total ordering makes exactly one successor valid. An attacker who compromises the current keys still cannot rotate, because they do not know the pre-committed next keys — only their digest.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/topic-a-the-onboarding-cost-question-1c7848.mem:359:1. **Prefer pre-rotation (KERI-style) over an overlap window if you can afford it.** It is the only surveyed model where the dual-authority hazard does not exist, and it makes single-sig → multi-sig a *rotation*, not a *migration*. **Confidence: 15/20.**
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/topic-a-the-onboarding-cost-question-1c7848.mem:383:| KERI pre-rotation eliminates the overlap window | 12/20 |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/docs/train-w-launch.mem:42:# ''BUILT: fleet join and the face-subject seam.'' Fleet join carries the root-signed `device-admit/v1` edge, gate carriage, sealed address secret, vessel face-key certificates and add-a-face grants on node and browser. `openJoinedFaceSession` and the pure grant ring stand. `lares fleet join offer|answer --face <i>` carries the charter, household admit, floor pins and, with `--doc-url`, the document URL in the root-signed answer (`fleet-join.ts`, `device-admit.ts`). The admit carriage carries its full sovereign sync route pin inside signed bytes; `nexus-contract.ts` preserves the anchor and prior-roll checks.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/docs/train-w-launch.mem:44:# ''LIVE: SEALED FACE GATE.'' Node and browser dialers open each vessel socket through attach, verdict and open. The pin selects `(hearth, Nexus, route, tier)`; `gateKnock` and `knockSegment` in `gate-bundle.ts` form the knock, the sealed presentation carries face authority, and `GateSessionChannel` opens after the verdict (`face-dial.ts`, `face-gate.ts`, `gate-session-channel.ts`). The floor announcement signs under its pinned bundle. A herm's `herm-relay-port.ts` accepts persona-KEL drops over HTTP and stands no socket; its floor gate answers the knock on the vessel's own gate server (`herm-carriage.ts`) at the Nexus whose quorum counts its `carry` (`nexus carry-for`, `nexus carry --floor-pin`, `nexus carry-take`).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/docs/train-w-launch.mem:70:Every vessel socket opens through the sealed face gate's attach, verdict and open sequence. The knock comes from the pin (`gateKnock`, `knockSegment` in `gate-bundle.ts`); `GateSessionChannel` opens after the verdict. A sealed face presentation and an Ed25519 gate verdict carry the exchange (`leaf-presentation.ts`, `ed25519-door.ts`, `face-dial.ts`). The herm relay port (`herm-relay-port.ts`) takes persona-KEL drops over HTTP and stands no socket; its floor gate answers the knock on the vessel's own gate server (`herm-carriage.ts`) at the Nexus whose quorum counts its `carry` (`nexus carry-for`, `nexus carry --floor-pin`, `nexus carry-take`). Fleet admission uses `lares fleet join offer|answer --face <i>`; the root-signed answer carries charter, household admit, floor pins and, with `--doc-url`, the document URL (`fleet-join.ts`, `device-admit.ts`).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/docs/train-w-launch.mem:88:| E · relay leg | `herm-relay-port.ts` serves persona-KEL drops over HTTP; `nexus carry-for`, `nexus carry --floor-pin` and `nexus carry-take` carry the floor pin | — |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-field-collision-ii.mem:64:''VINDICATION + the strongest single find: did:webvh.'' It is the ONLY system in the survey that pairs ''ledger-free pre-rotation'' (`nextKeyHashes` — commit the ''hash'' of your next authorized key, so a stolen current key cannot seize succession — identical in spirit to our `nextHandleKeyDigest`) ''WITH an in-protocol witness+watcher layer'': ''witnesses'' threshold-gate updates (`did-witness.json`); ''watchers'' are non-voting monitors that cache the DID Log and ''detect equivocation'' (https://identity.foundation/didwebvh/v1.0/). It also gives credible exit via `portable: true` + `alsoKnownAs` while the SCID (self-certifying id) survives a full host move. This is a near-twin of our handle-KEL + Meshpalace-watcher + severability design, arrived at independently — strong negentropy that we are on a real attractor.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-field-collision-ii.mem:204:* Second exposure: their pre-rotation is ''self-contained in the log'' (`nextKeyHashes`; "every multikey in the current updateKeys MUST have its hash in the previous entry's nextKeyHashes"). Ours pre-rotates the //handle key// (`nextHandleKeyDigest`) but authorization rides the ''injected `OwnerHeadResolver`'' — a verification-time board lookup. A stale or lax resolver passes a superseded member key; their in-log witness proof has no such availability dependency. The module honestly //names// the dependency rather than hiding it, but it is a dependency they don't carry.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:575:| Herm's floor transport key / serving key | Node boot/raise and transport setup; usually per boot/read, rotates only on policy/compromise. | ''F'' public pin/certificate and public transport configuration. If serving key is actually secret, ''S'' only for that node's local custody domain, never a browser VK shared by faces. Node serving key currently separately wrapped under VK: `vault-carriers.ts:239-251`; browser reads floor card before face admission `open-browser-vessel.ts:220-225,752-769,835-843`. Need distinguish public pin from secret serving authority. ''MEASURED current, LEAD classification.'' |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:677:| Herm floor transport key / serving key / transport configuration | Node raise/boot and transport setup; generally each boot, key rotation when explicitly required | ''F'' public pin/card/config; ''S only if'' secret serving authority really must be available before face unlock, scoped to node system custody | Floor rows and serving-tree distinction (`vault-carriers.ts:239-259`). Browser reads floor card/pins before face admission (`open-browser-vessel.ts:752-769,835-843`). Do not call a public pin a secret or bury a secret serving key in browser VK by analogy. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:948:For every row-bearing node/container type above, acts are filtered from the merged census: (1) found place / VK / slot tree → place signing seed ''S'' under today's implementation; public place/card/charter F; each PersonaGroup root/FCK/slot tree D; serving key/tree only becomes S if a serving payload is actually present; (2) fleet offer/join pending → public signed transport F, target face's pending secret/address/record D; (3) slot bind/remove/recovery → that face's route tree D; (4) roster, labels, declarations, handle-book → face-specific content D, minimal opaque public routing only F; (5) Keyhive/Veil archive, dyads, face proof/member and Nexus keys → D; (6) face backup/restore → D; (7) clear custody removes that face's rows and routes, while F survives; (8) floor card, public charter, public gate and public artifact carriage → F. Prior precise carrier/caller citations: `../PRIOR-YIN.md` census table and `../PRIOR-YANG.md` census table. Node carrier line inventory: `vault-carriers.ts:287-398`; rotation's current VK-carrier sweep: `vault-carriers.ts:477-510` (current mechanic to be replaced by face/row-aware rotation).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1086:| ''KERI / KERIpy'' | KERI establishment events publish current signing keys and commit by digest to next keys. Rotation needs current signatures and opens committed next keys; next private keys are the recovery boundary. KERI docs state pre-rotation helps mitigate later current-key compromise; if root pre-rotated keys are captured, no KERI-internal recovery exists. Sources: [[KERI 1.0 spec|https://trustoverip.github.io/kswg-keri-specification/]], [[KID0005 commentary|https://identity.foundation/keri/kids/kid0005Comment.html]], [[vLEI KERI infrastructure governance|https://www.gleif.org/media/pages/organizational-identity/become-a-vlei-issuer-qvi/vlei-ecosystem-governance-framework/23f7b8278d-1774607226/2025-04-16_vlei-egf-v3.0-technical-requirements-part-1-keri-infrastructure-2024_v1.3_final.pdf]]. KERIpy's keeper model has current/next public lots and rotation indexing ([[keripy keeping.py|https://github.com/WebOfTrust/keripy/blob/main/src/keri/app/keeping.py]]). | Pre-rotation key should be kept offline or under stronger threshold custody; its exposure is much more serious than current online signer exposure. A human need not be present for ordinary current-key signing, but recovery rotation needs access to next key(s). Pre-rotation cannot prevent forgery while stolen current key remains authoritative; it supplies verifiable path to supersede it. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1107:''LEAD recommendation: split a long-lived place root from a boot signer, and give the place identifier a KERI-like pre-rotated recovery path.'' The online signer may sign a tightly enumerated set of place acts and public floor-gate derivations. An offline owner-held root or threshold-held next key authorizes rotation of the online signer and publishes a verifiable KEL event. Keep at least one next key outside the always-on node. Use TPM/systemd encrypted credential as an optional at-rest wrapper for the online signer on supported nodes, with explicit fallback policy on systems lacking TPM.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1109:This third shape improves on plain clear-file candidate (1) by making stale online-key theft recoverable and machine-tied at rest where available. It improves on human-at-every-boot candidate (2) by preserving Herm public service and unattended container restart. It does ''not'' erase the theft window: attacker with current signer can forge until verifiers accept the rotation; attacker with next/root key can seize or permanently lose the place identity. If recovery cannot be implemented with independently protected next key, do not claim pre-rotation protection. Root/next compromise in pure KERI can require abandoning the identifier and re-founding identity with ecosystem-level rebinding.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1124:Detection: compare KEL histories / witnessed public floor records, keep tamper-evident receipts and independent public-key pins, and alert on conflicting valid histories or unexpected rotation. Recovery: rotate place key through authenticated KEL pre-rotation if next key remains safe; revoke/replace relying pins and re-found/rebind if recovery authority is also lost. Kapae/re-found semantics are house-specific and were not independently verified in this internet pass, so this mapping is ''LEAD'', not a measured procedure. A stolen current key enables forgery until rotation is accepted; pre-rotation does not retroactively erase that window.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1146:# ''Third shape: named place signer + separately protected offline/threshold place root and pre-rotated next key; optionally TPM/systemd-wrap online signer.'' Best blend for unattended public availability and recoverability. It still recognizes an online signer as a limited place authority. Herm/node can start at floor; a compromised online seed is revocable only while next/root authority is safe.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1164:Build one end-to-end adversarial test around a copied data disk and a no-human reboot: (a) on approved machine/boot, public place card and public relay start; every exact place signer domain is logged against an allowlist; (b) neither `vesselSeed` nor derived private child bytes appear in disk image, environment, logs, API, or public artifacts; (c) a different host cannot unwrap the credential; (d) no VK/FCK is present and direct attempts to open, replace, restore, rotate, or decrypt any face row fail byte-preservingly; (e) same-host restart does not admit a face; (f) attack-simulate theft of the current place signer and show an independently held pre-rotated key emits a valid KEL rotation, after which old-key place acts are rejected; (g) compromise of the offline next key is treated as identity-loss/re-found, not claimed recoverable. For candidate (2), add the expected deliberate tradeoff assertion: no person means no signer and no signed place identity at boot.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1208:* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' `node-vessel-identity.ts:312` signs the place KEL inception when founding. `packages/lararium-mesh/src/vessel-kel.ts:2-13,35-59` fixes the signed domain to `vessel-kel`, with an `icp` event containing one public key and its self-signature.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1209:* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' `packages/lararium-node/src/commands/nexus-contract.ts:250-268` uses the seed to sign `nexus carry-for`'s place carrier contract and, when a Nexus is present, the place floor-pin statement. These are place claims, not face signatures.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1216:* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' `packages/lararium-node/src/main.ts:765-769` uses vessel identity as the publisher on a floor card. The public card itself is a derived/public artifact; the signing seed does not need to appear in the card.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1228:* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' Boot loads the seed before floor and node composition (`open-node-vessel.ts:945-946`), then derives floor cards/gates and can establish relay and internal node signing capabilities. The `vesselFloorKeyDoor` explicitly answers while locked (`node-vessel-identity.ts:454-470`). A herm can therefore publish/serve the place floor without a face or a person present.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1230:* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' Fleet join, `carry-for`, founding, and promotion CLI paths occur under explicit commands; those can wait for a person. Daemon boot, floor gate reconstruction, boot-time floor card and internal signer setup cannot wait without changing the availability contract.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1239:* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' A seed thief can sign as the place key anywhere a relying party accepts that key: forge future place carrier seals/floor pins, device/place assertions, floor-card publications, and vessel-authenticated internal asks; derive relay and registered floor-gate children; recreate seed-derived founder veil identities given their derivation tags. The exact reachable operations depend on verifier policy and available public context.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1241:* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' The place KEL offers no recovery: `vessel-kel.ts:2-13` and `node-vessel-identity.ts:185-198` explicitly define non-transferable identity, no next-key commitment, and fresh founding after loss/theft. There is no place KEL rotation path in the assigned source; `rg` found only inception/decode/verify. `lares nexus seal rotate` rotates charter seats/epoch, not the place key. Vault `--rotate` rotates custody VK, not this seed.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1252:* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' Unlike the persona KEL or Nexus charter epoch chain, the place KEL has one self-signed inception and no `n`/next-key digest, threshold, sequence, or successor event. The module comments expressly reject a same-disk next key as offering no theft protection.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1253:* ''LEAD — 2026-10-19:'' A genuinely useful split would put a long-term place root offline and have it certify replaceable boot keys, with explicit expiry/revocation/recovery authority; merely certifying one static boot signer does not help if that signer cannot be revoked. But this changes all verifiers/pins and recovery semantics. A pre-rotation place KEL could make compromise recoverable only if the next private key stays outside the stolen boundary and all consumers enforce the chain.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1274:# ''LEAD — 2026-10-19 — Rank 3: boot custody door around the existing signer.'' It improves protection while powered off but breaks unattended herm/container/waking-floor service until a person arrives. Use only where operator presence at boot is an explicit availability choice. The existing place KEL still cannot rotate after a theft.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1282:''Red test — LEAD, 2026-10-19:'' From a clean persistent state, boot a faceless herm and restart its container with no human and no VK/SK/FCK. Public floor card, registered floor-gate children, and configured relay identity must reconstruct consistently; outputs/logs expose no seed. Assert every face/custody open and every operation requiring face authority refuses. Then copy only the at-rest image into an attacker-readable environment: the chosen protection must either withhold the signer (protected-store rank 1/2) or explicitly demonstrate/document the clear-file exposure; seed-only access must not unlock custody rows or face archives. For rank 2, additionally rotate the place boot key using the offline root and prove old-key signatures fail after consumers advance, while an offline stolen current seed cannot forge the successor chain.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1306:| Seed consumer inventory includes carry-for contracts/floor pins, asks, device/group-secret operations, relay gate derivation, carriage, CLI/founding, dyad founder veil derivation, and floor card. | ''CONFIRM, with scope caveat.'' Opened cited sites: `commands/nexus-contract.ts:250-268`; `open-node-vessel.ts:1217,1394,1450,2150,2279-2280,3087,3103,3115`; `commands/fleet-join.ts:285+`; `commands/init.ts:246-251,320-327,491-510`; `lares-cli/src/commands/meme.ts:1117`; `lararium-mesh/src/dyad.ts:399-402`; `main.ts:765-769`. TWIN's inventory is substantially more complete than the initial YIN handoff. Exact data reachable through the group-secret opener still requires separate record-level audit. | twin-alone |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1352:''MEASURED — 2026-10-19 — twin-alone:'' A reader of current seed can sign wherever public-key verifiers accept it: place carrier contract/floor pin, vessel/device assertions and internal asks; can derive registered floor-gate children and relay gate seed; and may reproduce seeded founder veil identities when derivation tags are known. The current key file is clear hex, mode 0600 (`node-vessel-identity.ts:140-170`).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1356:''MEASURED — 2026-10-19 — twin-alone:'' Existing place KEL provides no next-key commitment, rotation, or recovery. Therefore it offers no cryptographic way to distinguish legitimate from attacker signatures made with the same stolen seed. Independent transparency witnesses and authenticated pins could reveal inconsistent public histories, but are not in the measured place KEL.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1358:''LEAD — 2026-10-19 — yours-alone:'' Kapae may shadow or sever relationships under its own authority, but cannot undo the compromised signer or automatically revoke every consumer. Under current code the robust recovery path is re-found a new place identity, then re-pin/re-carry/update dependent gates and fleet relations. With an implemented pre-rotation chain, rotate using offline next authority, have consumers verify and advance, then reject old-key signatures; compromise of next/root means re-found. Forgery window persists until consumers accept rotation.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1367:''Rank 1 — LEAD — 2026-10-19 — confirmed-by-both: third shape, split root and boot signer with pre-rotated recovery.'' Keep an owner/threshold-controlled place root and committed next key offline; use a replaceable, narrowly scoped online place signer for unattended floor operations. Optionally protect its at-rest credential with TPM/systemd or OS keystore where available. Publish and verify a KEL-like rotation chain across all consumers. This improves theft recovery while preserving service. It is not a quick wrapper change: existing KEL and relying pins do not support this today, and current signer consumers must be narrowed/audited first.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1387:Run an adversarial no-human boot and copied-disk test. On the authorized host, a faceless Herm and restarted container must publish the expected place card/relay/gate identity; no seed or child secret appears in disk-visible public artifacts, logs, environment, or API. On a copied disk / wrong host, signer release fails if TPM/host binding was selected (or the documented owner-only file exposure is demonstrated if not). With no VK/FCK, attempts to read, replace, restore, rotate, or decrypt any face row fail byte-preservingly. Verify allowlisted signer domains only, and specifically assert that group-secret and dyad derivations cannot become broad custody opens. Then simulate current online-key theft: offline pre-rotated authority emits a valid successor; all consumers advance and reject old-key place acts. If offline next/root is also compromised, require re-found rather than claiming recovery. Candidate (2) variant must assert that no human means no signed place floor identity.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1418:| Place KEL can recover by pre-rotation. | ''REFUTE for current code.'' `lararium-mesh/src/vessel-kel.ts:1-17,38-45` explicitly says non-transferable, one-key inception, no next digest/sequence/version; stealing/loss requires re-founding. Charter seat KEL rotation is a different mechanism. | yours-alone |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1419:| Clear current key + offline pre-rotated successor is viable third shape. | ''CONFIRM as LEAD only.'' It is a redesign, not extant behavior; current parser/verifier rejects extra fields / next key. Needs schema, rotation, acceptance/pinning, and independently stored successor. | twin-alone |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1433:''MEASURED 2026-10-19:'' The system analogies describe at-rest and boot-release controls; none grants Lares row-key authority. The current mesh floor KEL does not possess KERI pre-rotation. Its format has exactly one self-signed `icp` key and rejects next-key commitment (`vessel-kel.ts:1-17,38-45`). Thus KERI's recovery guarantee cannot be mapped onto today's place key. Nexus charter/seat rotation and Kapae do not repair the place identity: they govern charter seats/participants, not floor KEL. Signal-like public transparency could help detect conflicting announcements, but no place witness/transparency path surfaced in the audited code.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1435:''MEASURED 2026-10-19:'' Candidate (1)'s narrow “place acts only” is not literally true today: seed reaches floor HKDF children, relay gate derivation, `carry-for` seal and floor pin, promotion assertion, device-enrolment signing/opening, and `deriveDyadVeil`; it is supplied into node/Keyhive setup. The red design boundary must inventory and type these uses. The floor gate itself is carefully separate from custody root: both tier doors refuse the other's domain (`gate-bundle.ts:250-310`; `node-vessel-identity.ts`), so the existing typed boundary supports “cannot open custody row,” not “no other credentials derived.”
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1437:''LEAD 2026-10-19:'' Map systemd encrypted credentials/TPM to wrapping the place signer at rest and unattended boot release. This may reduce copied-disk theft while preserving Herm uptime; it does not address compromise of a running host. Vault auto-unseal and Kubernetes secret mounts resemble delegated machine release and have similar runtime trust; plain Kubernetes Secret is not enough. Tor/SSH/WireGuard/ACME show how operators accept an always-on service identity and respond to theft by replacing/rebinding public identity. KERI pre-rotation is the closest recovery design only after adding the absent place-KEL machinery. Signal transparency is useful detection inspiration, not key protection.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1446:''Rank 1 — third shape: split long-lived place root from scoped boot signer, with independently protected pre-rotated successor. LEAD, 2026-10-19.'' Keep Herm's operational signer online, narrowly authorized for explicitly named floor/place domains. Let an offline owner-held root or threshold process authorize successor rotation; keep next seed off host. Optionally wrap online signer with TPM/systemd credential on supported systems. First remove or separately justify non-place uses (notably dyad veil/face enrollment). This preserves unattended public boot and adds a recovery path; it does not prevent forgery while a current signer remains accepted. Requires new KEL contract, verifier, rotation command, witness/pin update, and recovery runbook.
